        <Document ID="B78095E0-C090-4643-B128-37E56514F286">
            <Title>Section 5: Exploratory Data Visualizations</Title>
            <Text>Using the data, three graphs were developed to align with assignment requirements. Figure 1 is a scatter diagram showing salary to the player’s RBI, with the design developed by Benjamin Hays in the program Tableau, and coded by Tristen Garcia. Figure 2 is a bar chart showing the RBIs on the Y axis with the player’s birth months on the X axis. This was designed by Benjamin Hays in the program Tableau, and coded by Tristen Garcia. Figure 3 is [insert information here], designed and coded by Tristen Garcia with verbal assistance by Benjamin Hays. Details about the graphs and their meaning can be found in the file “report.md”, written and coded by Benjamin Hays with verbal assistance and revision by Tristen Garcia, located within the Python code. </Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="56216B66-EF47-4A40-BD7D-84DA82D422E7">
            <Title>References</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="AC58BF0A-5B02-4894-A652-44126004F905">
            <Title>Research Question 3 (RQ3)</Title>
            <Text>Research Question 3 (RQ3): Is there a connection between RBIs and player’s birth month? In other words, does the month the player was born in influence the total amount of RBIs in their career? To minimize data noise, only retired players will be analyzed for this question.
Hypothesis 3 (H3): The team believes there is a weak correlation between RBIs and birth months for MLB players. There are multiple variables that affect a player’s ability to hit RBIs, including the birth location, or high school location elevation. Additionally, a player’s genetic makeup may influence oxygen saturation level (see, for example, Gibbens [2018] article on the Bajau’s people ability to stay underwater for up to 13 minutes without physical harm).
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="0C26E4F7-8423-45FD-89D3-238135DF35EC">
            <Title>Section 6: Progression &amp; Research Question Alignment</Title>
            <Text>A second milestone is an opportunity to review if the research questions asked are aligned with the data. In other words, does the data exist to answer the questions, or would better questions be asked from the data. The chart was typed by Benjamin Hays, with verbal input from Tristen Garcia. See Table 6.1, below
Additionally, “main.py’ was developed and maintained by Benjamin Hays to provide interactions with the various scripts. Another script, “remove.py” was crudely developed by Benjamin Hays to remove any created files as part of the testing process, allowing the folder to quickly be “reset” to a default state. Additionally, Benjamin Hays formatted and standardized the tables within this document, but the content of the tables are the responsibility of the stated parties.
</Text>
        </Document>
